* ==================================================================
* tb_sar_logic_timing -- what the synchronous choice actually buys,
* measured rather than asserted (issue #11, DR-0008).
*
* GENERATED by design/sar-logic/gen_sar_logic.py -- do not edit.
*
* Four single-ended loops, identical except for a delay inserted
* between the comparator's analog decision and the controller
* sampling it. The delay stands in for everything that has to happen
* inside one 62.5 ns bit cycle before the latching edge: DAC settling,
* comparator regeneration, and logic propagation.
*   ok   0 ns    reference
*   lt  40 ns    the whole of the ~40 ns of per-cycle slack the
*                survey (Sec 4.2) claims a 62.5 ns bit cycle has,
*                spent in one place
*   xl  50 ns    80 % of the cycle consumed -- still correct
*   bad 70 ns    DELIBERATELY past the 62.5 ns cycle: the decision
*                arrives after the edge that was supposed to latch it
*
* The 50 ns point is not the survey's original guess: an earlier draft
* of this deck used 55 ns and measured a real failure (max|err| = 2
* LSB against a >= 1 LSB threshold). Bisecting against this SAME
* behavioural DAC/comparator model found the true boundary between
* 50 ns (exact) and 52 ns (max|err| = 1 LSB, already over the 0.5 LSB
* bound). 50 ns is kept as the bracket point precisely because it is
* the largest round number this model measures as exact, not because
* it was the original guess -- CLAUDE.md requires a measured number
* here, not an assumed one. DR-0010 records the gap between the
* survey's slack estimate and this measured boundary; the shortfall
* is attributed to this testbench's RC DAC-settling model (tau =
* 2.56 ns) consuming part of the delay budget the survey treated as
* separate, not to a synchronous-logic problem.
*
* `bad` is the negative control, and it is the reason the other three
* mean anything: its check asserts the conversion IS wrong. Without a
* case that must fail, an err == 0 result on the first three would not
* show that this testbench can detect a broken conversion at all.
*
* A fifth loop, `tie`, holds its input exactly on the free-MSB
* decision threshold (v_in = V_cm to the last bit) for the whole run.
* That is the near-metastable input a real comparator resolves slowly
* or wrongly. The claim under test is NOT that the code is right --
* on an exact tie either adjacent code is correct -- but that the
* conversion still COMPLETES on schedule: drdy keeps arriving exactly
* 16 clocks apart. That is the concrete form of the row
* spec/prior-art-survey.md Sec 4.3 gives synchronous logic ('a stalled
* decision corrupts one bit') against asynchronous ('a stalled
* decision stalls the whole conversion; needs a timeout/watchdog').
* ==================================================================

.param vref={vdd_val}
.param vcm={vdd_val/2}
.param vth={vdd_val/2}
.param lsbse={vdd_val/1024}
.model sarl_sw sw(vt={vdd_val/2} vh=0 ron=1 roff=1e12)

vclk clk 0 pulse(0 {vdd_val} 0 100p 100p 31.25n 62.5n)

